      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The university, named after the 13th‑century Franciscan theologian St. Bonaventure of Bagnoregio, reflects its founder’s Catholic heritage.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        27,
        28
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/St._Bonaventure,_New_York",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "After World War II, returning veterans flooded the campus, prompting rapid expansion to meet the GI Bill‑driven enrollment boom.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/St._Bonaventure,_New_York",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "New York State Route 417 cuts through the town, linking it to the Southern Tier Expressway and the nearby city of Olean.",
